      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\n**Certificate level:**\n- Number: R137/2012-NL1-15.01\n- Scheme: This is an OIML Certificate of Conformity — typically scheme A (basic) or B (with MAA). NMi as Issuing Authority NL1... The certificate says \"OIML Issuing Authority NL1\" which suggests MAA (scheme B). Actually, OIML-CS scheme B involves Issuing Authorities like \"NL1\". The number format \"R137/2012-NL1-15.01\" with NL1 indicates Issuing Authority. Scheme B certificates are issued under MAA framework by Issuing Authorities designated like \"NL1\". Let me use \"B\" since it mentions \"OIML Issuing Authority NL1\" and \"The notification of NMi Certin B.V. as Issuing Authority can be verified at www.oiml.org\". Yes, scheme B.\n- project_number: 14200712\n- page_total: 4\n- member_state: The Netherlands\n- date_issued: 30 January 2015 → 2015-01-30\n- oiml_issuer_id: NL1\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- name: NMi Certin B.V.\n- address_lines: [\"Hugo de Grootplein 1\", \"3314 EG Dordrecht\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: C. Oosterman\n- person_title: Head Certification Board\n- phone: +31 78 6332332\n- email: certin@nmi.nl\n- website: www.nmi.nl\n- oiml_issuer_id: NL1\n\n**Applicants:**\n- Flow Meter Group B.V., Meniststraat 5c, 7091 ZZ Dinxperlo, The Netherlands\n\n**Manufacturers:**\n- Same as applicant (label says \"Applicant and manufacturer\"): Flow Meter Group B.V.\n\n**Certified type:**\n- category: \"Gas meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"FMR\", \"FMR-Dual\"]\n- module_designation: Not applicable (mechanical gas meter, power supply not applicable) — maybe null or \"Not applicable\". I'll say null since not explicitly stated. Actually the power supply voltage is \"Not applicable\" — mechanical meter. I'll set module_designation to null... Hmm, the schema says \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. For a mechanical gas meter, modules don't apply. I'll omit or null. I'll omit.\n- description: \"A rotary piston gas meter\"\n\n**Model family:**\nTwo type variants: FMR and FMR-Dual. But the tables define many size/G-value variants. The models are combinations of meter type (FMR / FMR-Dual), cyclic volume V, and G-value. That's a lot of variants. \n\nThe tables are multi-dimensional: Table 2 (FMR) and Table 3 (FMR-Dual). The example in the rules for R137 shows extracting a 2-D table into matrix_tables and model_level with models keyed by size (G1.6, G2.5).\n\nHere the model identity: each row is a G-value within a volume V, for a meter type. Let me create model_ids like \"FMR G6\" or \"FMR/V=0.25/G6\". The G-value seems the primary size designator but G values repeat across volumes (e.g., G25 appears in V=0.25, 0.39, 0.61, 0.73, 1.16). Also FMR vs FMR-Dual both have G100 etc. So model_id needs to include type + volume + G-value.\n\nLet me enumerate Table 2 (FMR):\n\nV=0.25 (threaded, transfer 4004.325):\n- G6: Qmax 10, Qmin 0.25, Qt 0.5, ρmax 101 bar, Threaded, class 1.0\n- G10: Qmax 16, Qmin 0.25, Qt 0.8, ρmax 101, Threaded, class 1.0\n- G16: Qmax 25, Qmin 0.25, Qt 1.25, 101, Threaded, 1.0\n- G25: Qmax 40, Qmin 0.25, Qt 2, 101, Threaded, 1.0 or 1.5\n\nV=0.39 (transfer 2574.268):\n- G10: 16, 0.25, 0.8, 101, D 40 or 50, 1.0\n- G16: 25, 0.25, 1.25, 101, 40 or 50, 1.0\n- G25: 40, 0.25, 2, 101, 40 or 50, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G40: 65, 0.25, 3.2, 101, 40 or 50, 1.0 or 1.5\n\nV=0.61 (1638.163):\n- G16: 25, 0.25, 1.25, 101, 40 or 50, 1.0\n- G25: 40, 0.25, 2, 101, 40 or 50, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G40: 65, 0.25, 3.2, 101, 40 or 50, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G65: 100, 0.25, 5, 101, 40 or 50, 1.0 or 1.5\n\nV=0.73 (1378.968):\n- G16: 25, 0.2, 1.25, 101, 40 or 50, 1.0\n- G25: 40, 0.2, 2, 101, 40 or 50, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G40: 65, 0.2, 3.2, 101, 40 or 50, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G65: 100, 0.2, 5, 101, 40 or 50, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G100: 160, 0.4, 8, 12, 50 or 80, 1.0 or 1.5\n\nV=1.16 (861.8609):\n- G40: 65, 0.4, 3.2, 101, 50 or 80, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G65: 100, 0.4, 5, 101, 50 or 80, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G100: 160, 0.4, 8, 101, 50 or 80, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G160: 250, 0.65, 12.5, 12, 50 or 80, 1.0 or 1.5\n\nV=1.45 (689.484):\n- G65: 100, 0.6, 5, 101, 80 or 100, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G100: 160, 0.6, 8, 101, 80 or 100, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G160: 250, 0.6, 12.5, 101, 80 or 100, 1.0 or 1.5\n\nV=1.81 (552.8925):\n- G65: 100, 0.6, 5, 101, 80 or 100, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G100: 160, 0.6, 8, 101, 80 or 100, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G160: 250, 0.6, 12.5, 101, 80 or 100, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G250: 400, 1, 20, 12, 80 or 100, 1.0 or 1.5\n\nV=1.98 (504.5638):\n- G100: 160, 1, 8, 101, 80 or 100, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G160: 250, 1, 12.5, 101, 80 or 100, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G250: 400, 2.5, 20, 12, 80 or 100, 1.0 or 1.5\n\nV=3.17 (315.352):\n- G160: 250, 1.6, 12.5, 101, 80 or 100, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G250: 400, 1.6, 20, 101, 80 or 100, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G400: 650, 2.5, 32, 12, 80 or 100, 1.0 or 1.5\n\nV=5.15 (194.0625):\n- G250: 400, 2.5, 20, 101, 100 or 150, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G400: 650, 2.5, 32, 101, 100 or 150, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G650: 1000, 6.25, 50, 12, 100 or 150, 1.0 or 1.5\n\nThat's 4+4+4+5+4+3+4+3+3+3+3 = 40 rows for FMR.\n\nTable 3 (FMR-Dual):\n\nV=2.41 (414.462):\n- G160: 250, 1, 12.5, 21, 80 or 100, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G250: 400, 2.5, 20, 21, 80 or 100, 1.0 or 1.5\n\nV=3.96 (252.282):\n- G250: 400, 2.5, 20, 21, 100 or 150, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G400: 650, 4, 32, 21, 100 or 150, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G650: 1000, 4, 32, 12, 150 or 200, 1.0 or 1.5\n\nWait — for the third row of V=3.96: \"650 | 1000 | 4 | 32 | 12 | 150 or 200\". Hmm Qmax=1000 with Qt=4? That looks odd — Qmin 4, Qt 32? Let me re-read the columns: G-value, Qmax, Qmin, Qt, ρmax, D, revolutions, m⁻³, accuracy class.\n\nRow: `<td>650</td><td>1000</td><td>4</td><td>32</td><td>12</td><td>150 or 200</td><td>252,282</td><td>1</td><td>1,0 or 1,5</td>`\n\nSo G650, Qmax=1000, Qmin=4, Qt=32, ρmax=12, D=150 or 200. Hmm, Qmin=4 and Qt=32 for Qmax=1000 — that's odd since typically Qmin = Qmax/50 = 20, Qt = Qmax/10... Actually for G650 in FMR table: Qmax 1000, Qmin 6.25, Qt 50. Here for FMR-Dual 650: Qmax 1000, Qmin 4, Qt 32. Ratio 1000/4 = 250, 1000/32 ≈ 31. Hmm, unusual but that's what the table says. Actually wait — this could be OCR errors in the original, but I must extract as given. Actually it might genuinely be \"Qmin=4, Qt=32\"? Hmm, wait maybe columns: G-value 650, Qmax 1000, Qmin 4, Qt 32? Hmm... Actually possibly it's Qmax=1000, Qmin=... whatever, extract as-is.\n\nSimilarly V=6.34:\n- G400: 650, 4, 32, 21, 150 or 200, 157.676, 1, 1.0 or 1.5\n- G650: 1000, 6.5, 50, 21, 150 or 200, 157.676, 1, 1.0 or 1.5\n\nWait row: `<td>650</td><td>1000</td><td>6,5</td><td>50</td><td>21</td><td>150 or 200</td><td>157,676</td><td>1</td><td>1,0 or 1,5</td>` — G650, Qmax 1000, Qmin 6.5, Qt 50, ρmax 21.\n\nSo FMR-Dual has 7 rows: 2+3+2 = 7.\n\nTotal 47 model variants. That's a lot. The schema requires model_family.models with one entry per variant and matrix_tables to preserve the tables.\n\nApproach: \n- `matrix_tables[]` with two entries: \"Essential characteristics FMR\" (Table 2) and \"Essential characteristics FMR-Dual\" (Table 3), plus maybe the drums table (Table 4 — the minimum number of drums table).\n- `model_family` with family_name null (or \"FMR\" family? The cert names the family \"FMR and FMR-Dual\" — I could use family_name: \"FMR / FMR-Dual\" or null). I'll use null and include models list.\n- `characteristics.model_level` entries for qmax, qmin, qt, pmax, cyclic_volume, etc.\n\nModel IDs: I need to disambiguate. Options: \"FMR G6 (V=0.25)\", \"FMR-Dual G160 (V=2.41)\". Given G values repeat, include volume. I'll use format like \"FMR V0.25 G6\".\n\nActually, let me think about what's most usable. The R137 example uses size as model_id (G1.6, G2.5) because each size appears once. Here sizes repeat across volumes and meter types. A reasonable canonical model_id: \"FMR G6\" wouldn't be unique... actually let me check: is each (G-value, V) combination unique within a meter type? In FMR: G10 appears in V=0.25 and V=0.39. G16 in 0.25, 0.39, 0.61, 0.73. G25 in 0.25, 0.39, 0.61, 0.73, 1.16. Etc. So not unique. Model ID must include V and G: e.g., \"FMR/V=0.25/G6\".\n\nHmm, but perhaps also diameter D and transfer relationship are distinguishing... The transfer relationship (revolutions per m³) is derived from V. Diameter options vary by row too. Keep model_id = type + V + G.\n\nAlternatively, just key by G-value with two separate matrix_tables and set models by G-value plus attributes in model_level including volume... But volume varies per G too. Simplest robust: model_id = \"FMR G6 V0.25\" style.\n\nLet me define models array with entries:\n{\"model_id\": \"FMR V=0.25 G6\", \"label\": \"FMR, cyclic volume 0.25 dm³, G6\", \"attributes\": {\"meter_type\": \"FMR\", \"cyclic_volume\": 0.25, \"g_value\": \"G6\", ...}}\n\nHmm, but the schema says models[]: model_id, label, attributes{}. Putting attributes in the model entries duplicates model_level. The R137 example only used model_id without attributes. I'll include attributes with the per-model characteristics (meter_type, g_value, cyclic_volume, max_pressure_loss, diameter_options, transfer_relationship_revolutions, accuracy_classes) since that's the cleanest. Actually, the rules say to ALSO populate model_level. Let me do both:\n\n- model_family.models: list of model_id (+ maybe minimal attributes like meter_type and g_value and cyclic_volume).\n- characteristics.model_level: attributes × models matrix with entries for qmax, qmin, qt, pmax, diameter, transfer_relationship_revolutions, accuracy_class, cyclic_volume, g_value.\n\n47 models × ~9 attributes = a lot of JSON. That's acceptable but heavy. Alternatively I could keep matrix_tables as the primary preservation and do model_level for the main flow attributes (qmax, qmin, qt, pmax). The example did both matrix_tables AND model_level for the key flow attributes (not all columns — cyclic_volume, max_pressure_loss, pmax were in the table rows of matrix_tables only). So model_level doesn't need every attribute. I'll include model_level for: qmax, qmin, qt, pmax (max pressure), cyclic_volume, accuracy_class maybe, transfer relationship revolutions maybe. Let me include: cyclic_volume, qmax, qmin, qt, max_pressure_loss... wait ρmax here is maximum pressure in bar, not pressure loss. Column: \"maximum ρmax [bar]\". So attribute: maximum_pressure with unit bar.\n\nI'll do model_level entries: g_value (string), cyclic_volume (dm³), qmax (m³/h), qmin (m³/h), qt (m³/h), maximum_pressure (bar), accuracy_class (string like \"1.0\" or \"1.0 or 1.5\"), transfer_relationship_revolutions (1/m³), diameter_options (string).\n\nThat's 9 attributes × 47 models = 423 value objects. Heavy but fine. Hmm, maybe trim: diameter and revolutions are essentially functions of the model; including them in both matrix_tables rows and model_level is redundant but follows the example's spirit (example included qmax/qt/qmin only, leaving others in matrix table). To keep output manageable, I'll include in model_level: qmax, qmin, qt, maximum_pressure, and cyclic_volume. Plus g_value maybe. Let me include g_value as string values, and cyclic_volume. Accuracy class: I could include as a separate list of values \"1.0\" or \"1.0 or 1.5\" — I'll include it too. Diameter and revolutions stay only in matrix_tables.\n\nActually, let me reconsider output size. The JSON will be large regardless (matrix tables with 47 rows × ~10 fields). Let me be systematic and complete but avoid unnecessary duplication. I'll include model_level for: cyclic_volume, qmax, qmin, qt, maximum_pressure, accuracy_class. Skip g_value/diameter/revolutions in model_level (they're in matrix_tables and in model attributes).\n\nFor model_family.models entries, include attributes: {meter_type, g_value, cyclic_volume, diameter_options, transfer_relationship_revolutions_per_m3, accuracy_classes} — hmm that duplicates again. The example models entries were minimal: {\"model_id\": \"G1.6\"}. I'll keep models minimal with model_id only, maybe label. Label: \"FMR rotary piston gas meter, G6, V = 0.25 dm³\". That's informative without duplication of full attributes. Fine.\n\nNow the drums table (Table 4, unnamed — it's the table after the footnote on page 4):\n\n| meter size | Minimum number of drums before the comma | (second column unnamed — presumably \"after the comma\") | control-element [m³] |\n- G6: 5, 3, 0.0002\n- G10 – G65: 6, 2, 0.002\n- G100 – G650: 7, 1, 0.02\n\nThe header: \"Minimum number of drums\" spans 2 columns: \"before the comma\" and an empty second header (likely \"after the comma\"). The second column values 3, 2, 1 = digits after the comma. I'll name columns: meter_size, min_drums_before_comma, min_drums_after_comma, control_element_m3.\n\nThe footnote text: \"On the name plate of the rotary meter the cyclic volume can be given in two possible formats: 1. with two digits behind the comma as stated in table 2 and 3, or 2. with a number containing 6 significant digits. In this case a HF pulse value can be accurately derived from the spinning rotors with an optical sensor.\"\n\nThis is footnote for \"*\" on Volume columns. So footnotes[]: marker \"*\", text as above. And footnote_markers: [\"*\"] on cyclic_volume values? The marker is on the column header \"Volume*\". I could add footnote_markers to matrix table or to cyclic_volume model_level values. I'll add \"footnote_markers\": [\"*\"] to the cyclic_volume attribute level... but the structured value shape requires footnote_markers per value. Hmm, in model_level the value objects: {model, value, footnote_markers}. Adding \"*\" to all 47 cyclic_volume values is noisy. Alternative: put it in matrix_tables description/footnotes. The matrix_tables schema has footnotes[] field. I'll put footnote in table footnotes and note in description. For model_level cyclic_volume I'll skip the marker to reduce noise... Actually, correctness: rule 7 says when a value references a footnote, record the marker on that value. The Volume column header has *, so technically each volume value references it. I'll include footnote_markers: [\"*\"] on cyclic_volume values. Hmm, 47 entries with it. It's mechanical; fine. Actually let me reconsider — maybe keep it clean and put footnote marker only in matrix_tables.footnotes and mention in description. The rules prefer markers on values. I'll include them. It's just repetitive JSON.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider the size of the output. 47 models. matrix_tables: Table 2 has 40 rows × 9 fields; Table 3 has 7 rows; drums table 3 rows. model_family.models: 47 entries. model_level: 6 attributes × 47 values = 282 value objects. Total maybe ~700-900 lines of JSON. That's fine for this task.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether to include cyclic_volume in model_level given it's derivable from model_id and repeated. I'll include it — it's a key characteristic.\n\nType-level characteristics (Table 1, applies to both types):\n- destined_for_measurement: \"Gas volume\"\n- ambient_temperature_range: {min: -25, max: 55}, °C\n- gas_temperature_range: {min: -25, max: 55}, °C\n- orientation: \"Horizontal / Vertical up / Vertical down (all orientations)\" — string value\n- flow_direction: \"Uni-directional (indicated with arrow)\"\n- power_supply_voltage: \"Not applicable\" — string value \"Not applicable\"\n- software_identification: \"Not applicable\"\n\nThese go in characteristics.type_level.\n\nAccuracy classes: 1.0 and 1.5 → recommendation.accuracy_classes: [1, 1.5] or [\"1.0\", \"1.5\"]. I'll use [1.0, 1.5] as numbers. Hmm, accuracy classes for gas meters are 1 and 1.5. I'll use numbers [1, 1.5].\n\n**Recommendation:**\n- id: \"R137\" (canonical — \"R137\" not \"OIML R 137-1\"). The cert says R 137-1 (2012). Canonical id: \"R137\", edition: 1 (the -1 part indicates part 1, not edition). Hmm. R137-1 is part 1 of R137. Edition is (2012) → 2012. But schema says edition (int). The year is 2012 — OIML editions are years. I'll set edition: 2012. And note part? There's no field for part. The example in the prompt for R60: id \"R60\", edition int. For R137-1, I'd do id: \"R137\", edition: 2012. Maybe add amendment: null. Also could put part info... no field. I'll keep id \"R137\" and edition 2012. Scheme: \"B\" (Issuing Authority based MAA). Actually — is it scheme B? OIML-CS: Scheme A = basic certificate, Scheme B = MAA certificate. NMi Certin as \"OIML Issuing Authority NL1\" strongly indicates scheme B (MAA). The certificate number format R137/2012-NL1-15.01 with issuer NL1 is MAA format. I'll use \"B\".\n\nAccuracy classes array: [1, 1.5].\n\n**Test reports:**\n- \"No. NMi-14200712-02 dated 30 January 2015 that includes 36 pages.\" → id: \"NMi-14200712-02\", date: \"2015-01-30\", pages: 36, role: \"type_evaluation_report\".\n\n**Revision history:** none mentioned. Omit.\n\n**Footnotes:**\n- marker \"*\", text: \"On the name plate of the rotary meter the cyclic volume can be given in two possible formats: 1. with two digits behind the comma as stated in table 2 and 3, or 2. with a number containing 6 significant digits. In this case a HF pulse value can be accurately derived from the spinning rotors with an optical sensor.\"\n\nThe \"*\" appears in both Table 2 and Table 3 Volume column. I'll list once.\n\nAlso \"\\* See remark on next page.\" — that's the reference on page 3; the actual footnote is on page 4. Combine into one footnote.\n\n**Date format:** date_issued: \"2015-01-30\".\n\nNow let me carefully build model lists.\n\nFMR models (model_id format: \"FMR V=0.25 G6\"):\n\nV=0.25:\n1. FMR V=0.25 G6: qmax 10, qmin 0.25, qt 0.5, pmax 101, D \"Threaded\", rev 4004.325, class 1.0\n2. FMR V=0.25 G10: 16, 0.25, 0.8, 101, Threaded, 4004.325, 1.0\n3. FMR V=0.25 G16: 25, 0.25, 1.25, 101, Threaded, 4004.325, 1.0\n4. FMR V=0.25 G25: 40, 0.25, 2, 101, Threaded, 4004.325, 1.0/1.5\n\nV=0.39:\n5. FMR V=0.39 G10: 16, 0.25, 0.8, 101, 40/50, 2574.268, 1.0\n6. FMR V=0.39 G16: 25, 0.25, 1.25, 101, 40/50, 2574.268, 1.0\n7. FMR V=0.39 G25: 40, 0.25, 2, 101, 40/50, 1.0/1.5\n8. FMR V=0.39 G40: 65, 0.25, 3.2, 101, 40/50, 1.0/1.5\n\nV=0.61:\n9. FMR V=0