252.282):\n40. FMR-Dual V=3.96 G250: 400, 2.5, 20, 21, 100/150, 1.0/1.5\n41. FMR-Dual V=3.96 G400: 650, 4, 32, 21, 100/150, 1.0/1.5\n42. FMR-Dual V=3.96 G650: 1000, 4, 32, 12, 150/200, 1.0/1.5\n\nV=6.34 (rev 157.676):\n43. FMR-Dual V=6.34 G400: 650, 4, 32, 21, 150/200, 1.0/1.5\n44. FMR-Dual V=6.34 G650: 1000, 6.5, 50, 21, 150/200, 1.0/1.5\n\nTotal 47 models.\n\nNow the matrix_tables. Columns for Table 2/3: \n[\"cyclic_volume\", \"g_value\", \"qmax\", \"qmin\", \"qt\", \"maximum_pressure\", \"connection_diameter\", \"transfer_relationship_revolutions\", \"transfer_relationship_unit\", \"accuracy_class\"]\n\nHmm, the \"Basic transfer relationship\" spans two sub-columns: \"revolutions\" and \"[m⁻³]\". So revolutions value with unit m⁻³ (1/m³). I'll use column \"transfer_relationship_revolutions\" with unit \"m⁻³\"... Actually revolutions per m³ — unit [m^-3] means value is per cubic meter (number of revolutions per m³). I'll write unit_symbol \"1/m³\"? The table says [m⁻³]. I'll use \"m⁻³\".\n\ncolumn_units: [ \"dm³\", null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"bar\", \"mm\", \"m⁻³\", null ]\n\nRows: each row object with these keys. For G-value in FMR table it's \"G6\" etc.; in FMR-Dual table the G prefix was dropped by OCR (\"160\", \"250\"). I should normalize to \"G160\", \"G250\" since it's the G-value column. Yes — in Table 3, the first data column under \"G-value\" contains \"160\", \"250\" etc. which means G160, G250. I'll write \"G160\" for clarity. That's interpretation but clearly correct given FMR counterparts.\n\nDiameter: \"Threaded\", \"40 or 50\", etc. Keep as strings.\n\nAccuracy class: \"1.0\" or \"1.0 or 1.5\" as strings.\n\nThe footnote \"*\" on Volume: I'll add footnote_markers to the volume value in rows? Rows are plain objects, not structured values. The matrix_tables schema: rows[] (object per row), footnotes[]. I'll put footnote in footnotes array of each table with marker \"*\", and note in description. For model_level cyclic_volume I'll add footnote_markers: [\"*\"].\n\nHmm, decision: adding [\"*\"] to every cyclic_volume value object makes it verbose. Let me add it — it follows rule 7 strictly.\n\nActually wait, let me reconsider: maybe keep cyclic_volume out of model_level and rely on matrix_tables + footnote there. The R137 example put qmax/qt/qmin in model_level but NOT cyclic_volume/max_pressure_loss/pmax. Following that precedent exactly, I'll put in model_level: qmax, qmin, qt, maximum_pressure. And cyclic_volume stays in matrix tables (with footnote). Hmm but cyclic_volume is THE primary size attribute... The example deliberately limited model_level to flow characteristics. But rule says detect models; model_level is for varying attributes. Everything varies. The example shows it's acceptable to have model_level cover the main attributes while matrix_tables preserve full detail.\n\nI'll include model_level for: qmax, qmin, qt, maximum_pressure, cyclic_volume. I'll skip footnote markers on cyclic_volume values to avoid 47 repetitions... no wait, rule 7. Ugh. Let me just include footnote_markers: [\"*\"] on cyclic_volume values. It's fine.\n\nHmm, actually — alternatively I can put footnote_markers on the values in the tables... rows are objects, e.g. {\"cyclic_volume\": 0.25, ...}. Can't attach markers there without restructuring. Keep it simple: footnotes array in matrix_tables + footnote_markers on model_level cyclic_volume values.\n\nLet me decide model_id format. \"FMR V=0.25 G6\" — hmm, or \"FMR-0.25-G6\". I'll use \"FMR G6 (V=0.25 dm³)\"? Too verbose. Let me use \"FMR V=0.25 G6\". Clean and unambiguous.\n\nFamily name: null. Actually could set \"FMR\" family? The two types are FMR and FMR-Dual. family_name: null is safest.\n\nNow for model_level values, model references must match model_id exactly.\n\nLet me now also handle the drums table as matrix_tables[2]:\nname: \"Minimum number of drums and control element\"\ncolumns: [\"meter_size\", \"min_drums_before_decimal_point\", \"min_drums_after_decimal_point\", \"control_element\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, null, null, \"m³\"]\nrows:\n- {\"meter_size\": \"G6\", \"min_drums_before_decimal_point\": 5, \"min_drums_after_decimal_point\": 3, \"control_element\": 0.0002}\n- {\"meter_size\": \"G10 – G65\", ... 6, 2, 0.002}\n- {\"meter_size\": \"G100 – G650\", ... 7, 1, 0.02}\n\nThe header second sub-column was empty; values 3, 2, 1 with control element 0.0002 (4 decimals) — drums before comma 5, after comma 3: 0.000XX... control element = 10^-4? 0.0002 = 2×10⁻⁴. G10–G65: 6 before, 2 after → 0.002. G100–G650: 7, 1 → 0.02. The pattern: after-comma digits = 4 - before? 5→3, 6→2, 7→1. Sum 8? Hmm whatever. The empty header likely \"after the comma\". Given footnote says \"two digits behind the comma as stated in table 2 and 3\" — V values have 2 decimals. Hmm, the drums are of the cyclic volume counter. The second column label is likely \"behind/after the comma\". I'll name it \"min_drums_behind_comma\" to match text (\"behind the comma\"). And first \"min_drums_before_comma\". Use \"before_comma\"/\"behind_comma\"? I'll use \"min_drums_before_comma\" and \"min_drums_behind_comma\". control_element in m³.\n\nIs this table config-level? It varies by meter size — could be config_level, but matrix_tables is fine for preserving.\n\n**Components:** The meter is a mechanical rotary piston gas meter — no sub-assemblies named (drums/control element are counter internals, not really components). I'll omit components.\n\n**Characteristics type_level:**\n- measurement_quantity: \"Gas volume\" (from \"Destined for the measurement of\")\n- ambient_temperature_range: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit_symbol \"°C\"\n- gas_temperature_range: {min: -25, max: 55}, \"°C\"\n- orientation: \"Horizontal / Vertical up / Vertical down (all orientations)\"\n- flow_direction: \"Uni-directional (indicated with arrow)\"\n- power_supply_voltage: \"Not applicable\"\n- software_identification: \"Not applicable\"\n- installation_conditions: \"Not applicable\" — \"For this rotary meter specific installation conditions are not applicable.\" Could add as type_level: installation_conditions: {value: \"Not applicable\"}. I'll include it.\n\nStructured value objects need unit_symbol and unit_id keys. For string values: {\"value\": \"Not applicable\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}. For numeric: include unit_symbol and unit_id: null.\n\n**model_level structure:** entries: {attribute, unit_symbol, unit_id?, values: [{model, value, footnote_markers?}]}. The schema example shows {attribute, unit_symbol, values}. I'll include unit_symbol where numeric, omit unit_id (null default). Rule 2 says every characteristic value must be an object with value/unit_symbol/unit_id. So values entries: {\"model\": \"...\", \"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ..., \"unit_id\": null}? Looking at the R137 example:\n\n```\n{\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}, ...]}\n```\n\nUnit at attribute level, values are plain {model, value}. OK — I'll follow that: unit at attribute level. But rule 2's strict shape... the example in rule 4 contradicts slightly. The layered schema shows model_level[]: {attribute, unit, values[]: {model, value, footnote_markers[]}}. So unit at attribute level. Good, follow that.\n\nSo model_level:\n1. {attribute: \"cyclic_volume\", unit_symbol: \"dm³\", values: [{model, value, footnote_markers: [\"*\"]}...]}\n2. {attribute: \"qmax\", unit_symbol: \"m³/h\", values: [...]}\n3. {attribute: \"qmin\", unit_symbol: \"m³/h\", values: [...]}\n4. {attribute: \"qt\", unit_symbol: \"m³/h\", values: [...]}\n5. {attribute: \"maximum_pressure\", unit_symbol: \"bar\", values: [...]}\n6. {attribute: \"accuracy_class\", unit_symbol: null, values: [{model, value: \"1.0\"}...]} — hmm accuracy class as string \"1.0\" or \"1.0 or 1.5\". Keep as string.\n\nMaybe also g_value as string. And connection diameter and transfer relationship — leave in matrix_tables only. Actually let me include g_value too since it's the size designation; helps mapping. I'll include g_value (string values like \"G6\").\n\nThat's 7 attributes × 47 = 329 value objects. OK.\n\nHmm, this is getting really long. Let me just write it carefully.\n\nAlso accuracy_class values: \"1.0\" for first ones, \"1.0 or 1.5\" for most. In recommendation.accuracy_classes: [1, 1.5].\n\nNow the certified_type.type_designations: [\"FMR\", \"FMR-Dual\"].\n\ndescription: \"Rotary piston gas meter\" — from \"A rotary piston gas meter\". Use that.\n\ncategory: \"Gas meter\".\n\nCertificate:\n```json\n{\n  \"number\": \"R137/2012-NL1-15.01\",\n  \"scheme\": \"B\",\n  \"project_number\": \"14200712\",\n  \"page_total\": 4,\n  \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n  \"date_issued\": \"2015-01-30\",\n  \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n}\n```\n\nIssuing authority:\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"NMi Certin B.V.\",\n  \"address_lines\": [\"Hugo de Grootplein 1\", \"3314 EG Dordrecht\", \"The Netherlands\"],\n  \"person_responsible\": \"C. Oosterman\",\n  \"person_title\": \"Head Certification Board\",\n  \"phone\": \"+31 78 6332332\",\n  \"email\": \"certin@nmi.nl\",\n  \"website\": \"www.nmi.nl\",\n  \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n}\n```\n\nApplicants/manufacturers: same address as given (Meniststraat 5c, 7091 ZZ Dinxperlo, The Netherlands).\n\nNow write out all data. Let me carefully construct the FMR matrix rows.\n\nTable 2 rows (FMR). Columns: cyclic_volume (dm³), g_value, qmax (m³/h), qmin (m³/h), qt (m³/h), maximum_pressure (bar), connection_diameter (mm), transfer_relationship_revolutions (m⁻³), accuracy_class.\n\nRow format:\n{\"cyclic_volume\": 0.25, \"g_value\": \"G6\", \"qmax\": 10, \"qmin\": 0.25, \"qt\": 0.5, \"maximum_pressure\": 101, \"connection_diameter\": \"Threaded\", \"transfer_relationship_revolutions\": 4004.325, \"accuracy_class\": \"1.0\"}\n\nAll 40 FMR rows:\n1. {0.25, G6, 10, 0.25, 0.5, 101, Threaded, 4004.325, 1.0}\n2. {0.25, G10, 16, 0.25, 0.8, 101, Threaded, 4004.325, 1.0}\n3. 