x, qmin, qt, maximum_pressure. cyclic_volume in matrix tables with footnote there. Good — saves 44 objects and matches the example pattern.\n\nWait, but then footnote_markers never appear in model_level. Fine — matrix_tables[].footnotes carries it.\n\nNow matrix_tables:\n\nTable A:\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"Table 2: Essential characteristics FMR\",\n  \"columns\": [\"cyclic_volume\", \"g_value\", \"qmax\", \"qmin\", \"qt\", \"maximum_pressure\", \"connection_diameter\", \"transfer_relationship_revolutions\", \"accuracy_class\"],\n  \"column_units\": [\"dm³\", null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"bar\", \"mm\", \"m⁻³\", null],\n  \"rows\": [...37 rows...],\n  \"description\": \"Essential characteristics of the FMR rotary piston gas meter variants. Volume column marked * — cyclic volume may be given on the name plate in two formats (see footnotes).\",\n  \"footnotes\": [{\"marker\": \"*\", \"text\": \"On the name plate of the rotary meter the cyclic volume can be given in two possible formats: 1. with two digits behind the comma as stated in table 2 and 3, or 2. with a number containing 6 significant digits. In this case a HF pulse value can be accurately derived from the spinning rotors with an optical sensor.\"}]\n}\n```\n\nTable B: same for FMR-Dual with 7 rows.\n\nTable C: drums table.\n\nNow connection_diameter values: \"Threaded\" or \"40 or 50\" — strings.\n\nAlso note in FMR Table 2, the \"Diameter D [mm]\" for V=0.25 is \"Threaded\" (no diameter).\n\nNow the footnotes top-level: the \"*\" footnote. Include once in footnotes[]:\n{\"marker\": \"*\", \"text\": \"...\"}\n\nRecommendation:\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"R137\",\n  \"edition\": 2012,\n  \"amendment\": null,\n  \"scheme\": \"B\",\n  \"accuracy_classes\": [1.0, 1.5]\n}\n```\n\nHmm — the document says \"R 137-1 (2012)\". Part 1. Should I capture the part? No field for it. I could put id: \"R137-1\"? The rule says canonical e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". For R137-1, \"R137\" is the recommendation; -1 is the part. I'll use id \"R137\" and mention part in... hmm. Maybe keep id: \"R137\" — safe. Edition: 2012.\n\nTest reports:\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"NMi-14200712-02\",\n  \"date\": \"2015-01-30\",\n  \"pages\": 36,\n  \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"\n}\n```\n\nCertificate scheme: \"B\". Let me double check: OIML-CS introduced 2017... this cert is from 2015, before OIML CS formalization, but under MAA (the number format with NL1 and \"OIML Issuing Authority\" indicates MAA). The layered schema asks scheme (A|B). NL1 = Dutch Issuing Authority under MAA → B. Good.\n\npage_total: 4. member_state: \"The Netherlands\". oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\".\n\nNow, one more check on Table 3 row 5: \"650 | 1000 | 4 | 32 | 12\". Qmax=1000, Qmin=4, Qt=32?? Wait, hold on: could this row be G650: Qmax=1000, Qmin=... Let me compare FMR G650 (V=5.15): Qmax 1000, Qmin 6.25, Qt 50. And FMR-Dual V=6.34 G650: 1000, 6.5, 50. So V=3.96 G650 with 1000, 4, 32 — hmm inconsistent ratios but perhaps the dual meter measures differently. Actually wait — maybe this is a lower-capacity variant: for a 3.96 dm³ dual meter, G650 but reduced flows: Qmax 1000? That doesn't fit either. Whatever — extract as-is: qmin 4, qt 32.\n\nHmm, actually could OCR have merged/mangled \"20\"→\"4\" and \"32\"? Not my problem; extract as given. Actually wait, let me re-read: `<td>650</td><td>1000</td><td>4</td><td>32</td><td>12</td>...` — but hold on, maybe Qmin is 4 and Qt is... 32 doesn't fit Qt=Qmax/31. For G650 FMR-Dual V=3.96: hmm. Actually maybe these are correct: R137-1 requires Qmin ≥ Qmax/... for class accuracy... For rotary meters Q3/Q1 ratios up to 250? Qmax/Qmin = 250, Qmax/Qt ≈ 31.25. Odd but plausible for a dual (two-phase) meter where split range: low range 4–32? Hmm, actually \"Dual\" meters have two measuring carriages. Anyway, extract as printed.\n\nAlso V=2.41 G160: qmin 1, qt 12.5 → Qmax/Qt = 20, Qmax/Qmin = 250. Fine.\n\nNow assemble everything. I'll write the JSON compactly but valid.\n\nOne more consideration — \"characteristics\" object: type_level + model_level. Include model_level with 4 attributes.\n\nAlso should I add config_level? No config axes here. Omit.\n\nComponents: none. Omit.\n\nRevision history: none. Omit.\n\nFootnotes top-level: include the \"*\" footnote. Also maybe \"See remark on next page\" is not a footnote itself. Just \"*\".\n\nLet me also double-check Table 1 applies to both types: \"Table 1: FMR and FMR-Dual\" — yes, type_level.\n\nSoftware identification: Not applicable. Power supply voltage: Not applicable — a fully mechanical meter. Good.\n\nNow write the final JSON. Order: certificate, issuing_authority, applicants, manufacturers, certified_type, model_family, characteristics, matrix_tables, recommendation, test_reports, footnotes.\n\nLet me write models with labels:\n\nI'll write model entries as {\"model_id\": \"FMR V=0.25 G6\", \"label\": \"FMR G6, cyclic volume 0.25 dm³\"}. Hmm, that doubles size (~44 labels). The example omitted labels. I'll omit labels to keep it cleaner — model_id is self-describing.\n\nFinal JSON construction:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R137/2012-NL1-15.01\",\n    \"scheme\": \"B\",\n    \"project_number\": \"14200712\",\n    \"page_total\": 4,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2015-01-30\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi Certin B.V.\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Hugo de Grootplein 1\", \"3314 EG Dordrecht\", \"The Netherlands\"],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"C. Oosterman\",\n    \"person_title\": \"Head Certification Board\",\n    \"phone\": \"+31 78 6332332\",\n    \"email\": \"certin@nmi.nl\",\n    \"website\": \"www.nmi.nl\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Flow Meter Group B.V.\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Meniststraat 5c\", \"7091 ZZ Dinxperlo\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"manufacturers\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Flow Meter Group B.V.\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Meniststraat 5c\", \"7091 ZZ Dinxperlo\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Gas meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"FMR\", \"FMR-Dual\"],\n    \"module_designation\": null,\n    \"description\": \"Rotary piston gas meter\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": null,\n    \"models\": [ ... 44 entries ... ]\n  },\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {...},\n    \"model_level\": [...]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [...],\n  \"recommendation\": {\n    \"id\": \"R137\",\n    \"edition\": 2012,\n    \"scheme\": \"B\",\n    \"accuracy_classes\": [1, 1.5]\n  },\n  \"test_reports\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"NMi-14200712-02\",\n      \"date\": \"2015-01-30\",\n      \"pages\": 36,\n      \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"footnotes\": [\n    {\"marker\": \"*\", \"text\": \"On the name plate of the rotary meter the cyclic volume can be given in two possible formats: 1. with two digits behind the comma as stated in table 2 and 3, or 2. with a number containing 6 significant digits. In this case a HF pulse value can be accurately derived from the spinning rotors with an optical sensor.\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nmodule_designation: for a mechanical gas meter, \"Not applicable\" seems right. I'll set \"Not applicable\" since power supply is not applicable... Actually module designation refers to modules per R137? R137 gas meters don't use the module concept like Measuring Instruments Directive. Leave null. Hmm, either is defensible; null is safer (\"don't fabricate\").\n\nNow write all models:\n\nmodels:\n{\"model_id\": \"FMR V=0.25 G6\"},\n{\"model_id\": \"FMR V=0.25 G10\"},\n{\"model_id\": \"FMR V=0.25 G16\"},\n{\"model_id\": \"FMR V=0.25 G25\"},\n{\"model_id\": \"FMR V=0.39 G10\"},\n{\"model_id\": \"FMR V=0.39 G16\"},\n{\"model_id\": \"FMR V=0.39 G25\"},\n{\"model_id\": \"FMR V=0.39 G40\"},\n{\"model_id\": \"FMR V=0.61 G16\"},\n{\"model_id\